# OTP manual page

.TH OTP 1 "2026-08-23" "otp-toolkit v1.7.1" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
otp \- simple one-time pad encryption/decryption utility

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otp
[-h | --help]
.br
.B otp
[-nk | --new-key-pair] <size_in_MB> <part_a_name> <part_b_name>
.br
.B otp
[-ac | --add-contact] <contact_name> [<encryption_key_file> <decryption_key_file>]
.br
.B otp
[-rc | --remove-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-hc | --has-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-lc | --list-contacts]
.br
.B otp
[-sc | --show-contact] <contact_name>
.br
.B otp
[-c | --contact] <contact_name> --encrypt [--with-ack-file]
.br
.B otp
[-c | --contact] <contact_name> --decrypt [--with-ack-file]
.br
.B otp
[-st | --status] <contact_name> [--porcelain]
.br
.B otp
[-rl | --recover-last] <contact_name> --sent|--received
.br
.B otp
--add-rand-to-vault <size_in_MB>
.br
.B otp
[-x | --xor] <bytes>

.SH DESCRIPTION
This program takes data from standard input, XORs it with one-time-pad key material held in a keychain of contacts, and writes the result to standard output.  Consumed key material is removed from the key files automatically, so a spent key range can never be reused.  It also allows generating key pairs and managing the keychain of contacts.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display this help message and exit.

.TP
.B \-nk, \-\-new-key-pair
Generate a new key pair.  The key material is read from standard input and each party's pair of keys is written into its own directory, each file named for the correspondent it is used with: ``<part_a_name>_keys/encryption_for_<part_b_name>.key`` and ``<part_a_name>_keys/decryption_from_<part_b_name>.key``, and likewise ``<part_b_name>_keys/encryption_for_<part_a_name>.key`` and ``<part_b_name>_keys/decryption_from_<part_a_name>.key``.  Each directory holds exactly what that party receives: the key it uses to encrypt messages sent to the other party, and the key it uses to decrypt messages received from the other party.  The first argument is the size in megabytes (e.g., ``5`` for 5 MiB).  Standard input must be a pipe or a file: when it is a terminal (no randomness source piped in), the command checks the randomness vault (.keychain/_randomness, filled by --add-rand-to-vault) instead of failing outright.  If the vault holds at least twice the requested size (a pair draws two independent pads), it prompts ``Use randomness vault for key generation? [y/N]``; answering yes draws the pads from the vault through the same crash-safe stage/verify/publish-then-truncate machinery a contact's key file gets consumed by, answering anything else cancels with the vault untouched.  If the vault is missing, empty, or too small, the command fails immediately, before creating any directory or file, the same way a bare terminal always did.  Example: cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 5 partA partB

.TP
.B \-ac, \-\-add-contact <contact_name> [<encryption_key_file> <decryption_key_file>]
Add a new contact to the keychain.  Optionally specify encryption and decryption key files to load keys from disk (e.g., files generated by --new-key-pair).  The two key files must hold key material that does not overlap, and are compared by content rather than by name or size: one pad serving both directions would mean the range encrypting an outgoing message also decrypts an incoming one.  Refused are an identical pair, a copy under a different name, and a pad paired with part of itself - either its leading bytes or, as in a partially consumed key, its trailing bytes.  Re-using a contact name that has been used on this keychain before produces a warning, because freshly supplying the original key from generation would restart over already-spent bytes.  Beyond the warning, a key file whose content includes the recorded head of key material this keychain has already consumed - the original distribution copy of a spent key always does, whatever the contact is now called - is refused outright, using the \fBspent_heads\fP registry (see File Storage Architecture); supply only key material that has never been used, or the partially consumed remainder file itself.  If no key files are specified, the contact is created with empty keys.  Creates the contact's own metadata file (.keychain/<contact_name>.meta) with the new contact entry.  Each contact stores encryption/decryption keys, message sequence information, and retry counts.

.TP
.B \-rc, \-\-remove-contact <contact_name>
Remove a contact from the keychain.  Deletes the contact's metadata file and removes all associated data for the specified contact.

.TP
.B \-hc, \-\-has-contact <contact_name>
Check if a contact exists in the keychain.  Returns exit code 0 if the contact exists, 1 otherwise.

.TP
.B \-lc, \-\-list-contacts
List all contacts stored in the keychain.  Displays the names of all contacts found under the .keychain/ directory.

.TP
.B \-sc, \-\-show-contact <contact_name>
Show detailed information about a specific contact.  Displays all contact fields including encryption/decryption key sizes (keys themselves are masked for security), offsets, sequence numbers, timestamps, and retry counts.

.TP
.B \-c, \-\-contact <contact_name> \-\-encrypt
Encrypt data from stdin using the contact's encryption key and write the cipher text to stdout.  An encrypted metadata block (source_id, sequence, offset \- see ORIGIN AND ORDER VERIFICATION) is prepended to the message inside the ciphertext.  The encryption key is consumed and updated to contain only the remaining unused bytes.  Updates the contact's sequence number, encryption key offset, and sets the LastMessageSentAt timestamp.

.TP
.B \-c, \-\-contact <contact_name> \-\-decrypt
Decrypt data from stdin using the contact's decryption key and write the plain text to stdout.  The message's metadata block is validated against this contact's key state before any key material is consumed \- an invalid message is rejected with a distinct exit status (see EXIT STATUS) \- and is stripped from the output, which is exactly the original plaintext.  The decryption key is consumed and updated to contain only the remaining unused bytes.  Updates the contact's sequence number, decryption key offset, and sets the LastMessageReceivedAt timestamp.

.TP
.B \-y, \-\-assume-delivered
Skip the delivery-confirmation prompt when encrypting or decrypting with a contact.  Each direction's messages must be processed in the exact order they were sent, complete, exactly once; the per-message metadata layer rejects violations at decrypt time before any key is spent, but only the correspondents can confirm, out of band, that a delivered message actually reached its reader, so before spending key on any message after a direction's first, otp asks on the terminal (never on stdin, which carries the message itself) whether the previous message arrived and decoded correctly, and cancels with the keys untouched unless answered yes.  Pass this flag (or set the OTP_ASSUME_DELIVERED environment variable to 1) once delivery has been confirmed out of band; scripts must do so explicitly, because with no terminal available the operation fails closed rather than assuming delivery.

.TP
.B \-\-with-ack-file
Optional, alongside \fB-c <contact_name> --encrypt\fP or \fB--decrypt\fP.  Write that message's source_id, as 32 lowercase hex characters followed by a newline, to \fB<contact_name>_<seq>_ack_ref.sent.txt\fP on encrypt or \fB<contact_name>_<seq>_ack.received.txt\fP on decrypt, in the working directory, and print on standard error what the value is for.  The source_id is the 16-byte chunk at the head of the message's key range (see ORIGIN AND ORDER VERIFICATION): it is spent with that range but never used as pad, so disclosing it after the fact reveals nothing that protected the plaintext, while only a holder of the mirrored key at that exact offset could ever produce it.  That is what makes it usable as a delivery reference - the recipient decrypts with this flag and sends their value back in the clear; a sender whose file matches knows that exact message was decrypted by the holder of the mirror key.  It confirms decryption, not that a human read the message, and it is not a receipt provable to a third party, since the sender knows the value too.  Both correspondents must agree to acknowledge this way; nothing in the protocol requires or enforces it.  The file is written, verified and atomically published \fBbefore\fP any key material is spent, and a failure to write aborts the operation with nothing consumed - no ciphertext, no key spent, non-zero exit.  This ordering is not incidental: the source_id lives only in the key range the message is about to destroy, so a run that spent the key and then failed to record the reference would leave it unrecoverable, neither in the truncated key file nor in the ciphertext, whose metadata is sealed under pad bytes from that same range.  Writing first is safe because the value depends only on the key file and the current offset, never on the message: a run interrupted, or cancelled at the delivery-confirmation prompt, after the file is published spends nothing, and the retry republishes a byte-identical file.  A run that redelivers an earlier message (exit 8) writes no new reference and names the file the interrupted run had already published.

.TP
.B \-st, \-\-status <contact_name> [\-\-porcelain]
Report the contact's per-direction state, verified from the disk files themselves: the key file's physical size is the authority on remaining key material (never the metadata alone), the metadata declarations are cross-checked against it, pending crash-recovery artifacts are classified by the same code recovery itself runs, and the kept last-payload copies indicate whether the last sent/received message still awaits delivery confirmation.  With \fB--porcelain\fP, prints stable key=value lines (contact, and per direction: sequence, key_remaining, meta_state, redelivery_pending, ack_outstanding) for scripts.  Strictly read-only: it never deletes, truncates, renames or heals anything; the keychain directory is byte-identical before and after.  See EXIT STATUS and EXTERNAL INTEGRATION.

.TP
.B \-rl, \-\-recover-last <contact_name> \-\-sent|\-\-received
Stream the kept safety copy of the last delivered payload to standard output: \fB--sent\fP re-emits the exact ciphertext of the last encrypt (the re-transmission source when a message was lost in transit - the same bytes remain valid for the receiver's current key position; never re-encrypt), \fB--received\fP the exact plaintext of the last decrypt (the only remaining decryption once its key bytes are destroyed).  Read-only and repeatable: the copy is never deleted by this command, not even after a fully successful stream - only the next operation in the same direction removes it, when delivery is confirmed (interactive yes or \fB-y\fP).  See EXIT STATUS.

.TP
.B \-\-add-rand-to-vault <size_in_MB>
Read <size_in_MB> megabytes of randomness from standard input and store it in the keychain's randomness vault (.keychain/_randomness): appended to the end of the existing file if the vault already exists, or created with mode 0600 if it does not.  The bytes are stored exactly as read, with no transformation.  Standard input must be a pipe or a file, with the same terminal-refusal behavior as \fB--new-key-pair\fP.  The vault is not assigned to any contact and, at this point, is not itself consumed or tracked as key material - it exists purely as accumulated storage.  It is still guarded like a contact: an exclusive \fBflock()\fP on .keychain/_randomness.lock serializes concurrent invocations, and the incoming bytes are staged and read-back verified in full before the vault itself is ever touched, so a short read from stdin leaves it unchanged rather than partially appended.  On success, prints OK followed by a blank line and a summary of how much was just added and the vault's new running total, both in megabytes and exact bytes.  Example: cat /dev/urandom | otp --add-rand-to-vault 100

.TP
.B \-x, \-\-xor <bytes>
XOR standard input against <bytes> \- any readable file or character device \- starting at its offset 0, and write the result to standard output.  Raw plumbing, deliberately outside the keychain: <bytes> is only read, never truncated, and no metadata block, sequence number, source_id or delivery confirmation is involved \- so nothing here prevents the same key bytes from covering a second message, which is exactly the reuse that collapses one-time-pad secrecy (see SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS).  Use \fB-c <contact> --encrypt\fP/\fB--decrypt\fP for key material the keychain should account for; use this for keys managed entirely by the caller, and for combining randomness sources.  XOR is its own inverse, so the same invocation encrypts and decrypts.  Input and key are streamed in 4 MB chunks, so neither is ever held in memory whole and <bytes> may be a character device such as \fB/dev/urandom\fP or \fB/dev/hwrng\fP.  Exits 1 if <bytes> is shorter than the input, naming how many bytes were XORed before it ran out; whole chunks already written to stdout stand.  Example, mixing two independent randomness sources into the vault: cat /dev/urandom | otp --xor /dev/hwrng | otp --add-rand-to-vault 10 \- XORing independent sources yields randomness at least as unpredictable as the strongest of them, so a biased or compromised source cannot weaken the result (two draws from the same source add nothing).

.SH USAGE

.SS Using the Keychain System
To encrypt using a contact's key from the keychain:
.PP
.B echo "plain text" | otp -c contact_name --encrypt > cipher.txt
.PP
To decrypt using a contact's key from the keychain:
.PP
.B cat cipher.txt | otp -c contact_name --decrypt > plain.txt
.PP
The keychain automatically manages key consumption and updates the contact's metadata; the remaining key is stored in the keychain.

.SS Raw XOR, outside the keychain
To XOR standard input against a file of bytes you manage yourself:
.PP
.B cat data | otp --xor key.bin > data.xored
.PP
The same invocation reverses it, XOR being its own inverse:
.PP
.B cat data.xored | otp --xor key.bin > data
.PP
Nothing is consumed or recorded: key.bin is byte-identical afterwards, and it is entirely on you never to cover a second message with the same bytes.  Because the key may be any readable file, including a character device, this is also how independent randomness sources are combined:
.PP
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --xor /dev/hwrng | otp --add-rand-to-vault 10
.PP
Each stage draws from its own source, so the vault receives randomness at least as unpredictable as the strongest of them.

.SH NEW KEY PAIR GENERATION
Use the \fB-nk\fP or \fB--new-key-pair\fP flag to generate a new key pair from a source of randomness.
Example:
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 1 alice bob

.SH KEYCHAIN MANAGEMENT
The keychain feature allows you to manage contacts with their associated encryption and decryption keys.  Each contact's metadata and key files all live under the \fB.keychain/\fP subdirectory in the current directory.  The keychain provides a convenient way to perform one-time pad encryption and decryption with stored keys, automatically managing key consumption and metadata.

.SS Two Keys Per Contact, Mirrored Between the Parties
Every contact holds a key \fBpair\fP, never a single key: \fB<contact>_enc.key\fP is consumed only when you send to that contact, \fB<contact>_dec.key\fP only when you receive from them.  The pair is shared with the other party \fBwith the two roles inverted\fP - your encryption key is byte-identical to their decryption key, and their encryption key is byte-identical to yours (\fB--new-key-pair\fP lays the files out this way; each party's directory holds exactly its own two roles).  The file you consume to encrypt is the same bytes the other party consumes to decrypt.
.PP
The reason for a pair rather than one shared key is parallel, non-blocking messaging: the two directions consume different files tracked by separate offsets and sequence numbers, so both parties can send at the same time without coordinating and without their messages colliding on the same key bytes.  There is no protocol-level coordination between the two machines at all.  The independence is between the two directions, not within one: each direction's messages must still be decrypted in the exact order they were sent, complete, exactly once, which is what the delivery-confirmation prompt (see \fB-y\fP) makes each operator vouch for.  Because the directions are independent they also exhaust independently - a chatty sender can run out of encryption key while its decryption key is nearly full; \fB--show-contact\fP reports both remaining sizes separately.

.PP
.B File Storage Architecture
.br
The keychain uses a streaming architecture that supports extremely large keys (up to 1 TB) without loading them into memory.  The 1 TB range applies to 64-bit builds; a 32-bit build can only address key files up to 4 GiB minus one byte, and refuses a larger one with an explicit "too large for this build" error before any key material is spent.  The limit is per build, not per keychain: the same keychain files work on a 64-bit build up to the full 1 TB. Everything lives under \fB.keychain/\fP, one set of files per contact:
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>.meta\fP \- that contact's metadata (key file paths, offsets, sequences, timestamps), written and committed independently of every other contact's file
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>_enc.key\fP and \fB<contact>_dec.key\fP \- that contact's actual key files
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>.lock\fP \- a per-contact lock file used to serialize concurrent operations on the same contact
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>.last_sent\fP and \fB<contact>.last_received\fP \- an exact copy of what the last encrypt (ciphertext) or decrypt (plaintext) wrote to stdout, kept so a forgotten redirect cannot lose a message whose key bytes are already destroyed.  Removed automatically by otp - the user never has to delete it by hand - when the next operation in that direction confirms delivery (interactive "yes" or -y/--assume-delivered); when delivery is rejected instead, otp offers to recover the copy to a file of your choosing.  Deleted together with the contact.
.IP \(bu 3
\fBspent_heads\fP \- the spent-key registry, one for the whole keychain.  Records a fingerprint (two 64-bit digests, never key bytes) of each key's opening 64 bytes the first time they are consumed, so that re-supplying an original, already-spent key under a new contact name is refused even after the original contact and its key files are gone.  Never deleted.
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>_<dir>_pending_seq<N>_off<O>_len<L>.bin\fP (where <dir> is enc or dec) \- a finished, verified message awaiting delivery, its name tagged with the exact key range it used.  Deleted once delivered; if one survives a crash, the next operation on that contact uses the name tag to recover deterministically and redeliver it (see EXIT STATUS, code 8).
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>_<dir>_pending.<pid>.tmp\fP \- output still being staged by an in-flight operation, not yet verified.  One left behind by a crash carries no recoverable state and is swept away by the next operation on that contact.
.IP \(bu 3
\fB<contact>_enc.key.tmp\fP, \fB<contact>_dec.key.tmp\fP and \fB<contact>.meta.tmp\fP \- staging copies of a key or metadata file being rewritten, verified before being renamed over the real one.  Cleaned up automatically.
.PP
Finding one of the transient files after a crash or an interrupted run is normal: every one of them is either recovered or cleaned up automatically by the next otp command on that contact, and none of them is ever the only copy of anything that matters.
.IP \(bu 3
Keys are read and processed in 4 MB chunks during encryption/decryption, enabling support for terabyte-sized keys without memory constraints
.IP \(bu 3
Everything otp creates inside \fB.keychain/\fP is created mode 0600, key files included
.IP \(bu 3
A contact's name is used verbatim as a filename, so it may not be \fB.\fP or \fB..\fP, contain a path separator, any of \fB: * ? " < > | =\fP, or control characters

.SS Contact Data Structure
Each contact in the keychain stores:
.PP
.B Name
\- The contact's name (unique identifier)
.br
.B EncryptionKey
\- Encryption key stored in .keychain/<contact>_enc.key (displayed as ******* with remaining bytes for security). This key is used when encrypting messages to send to the contact. The key file is read in streaming chunks during encryption.
.br
.B EncryptionKeyOffset
\- Number of bytes consumed from the encryption key file. This tracks cumulative consumption for reference purposes.
.br
.B EncryptedSequence
\- Sequence number incremented on every encryption operation. Tracks the total number of messages encrypted (sent) to this contact.
.br
.B DecryptionKey
\- Decryption key stored in .keychain/<contact>_dec.key (displayed as ******* with remaining bytes for security). This key is used when decrypting messages received from the contact. The key file is read in streaming chunks during decryption.
.br
.B DecryptionKeyOffset
\- Number of bytes consumed from the decryption key file. This tracks cumulative consumption for reference purposes.
.br
.B DecryptedSequence
\- Sequence number incremented on every decryption operation. Tracks the total number of messages decrypted (received) from this contact.
.br
.B RetryCount
\- Counter for message retry attempts (range: 0-25000). Can be used to track retransmission attempts.
.br
.B LastMessageSentAt
\- UTC timestamp of when the last message was encrypted and sent. Updated only during encryption operations.
.br
.B LastMessageReceivedAt
\- UTC timestamp of when the last message was decrypted and received. Updated only during decryption operations.
.PP

.SS How Keychain Encryption Works
The keychain-based encryption follows these steps:
.PP
.B 1. Key Pair Generation
.br
Generate matching key pairs for two parties (e.g., Alice and Bob) using --new-key-pair. This creates two directories with two files each: alice_keys/encryption_for_bob.key (Alice's encryption key = Bob's decryption key) and alice_keys/decryption_from_bob.key (Alice's decryption key = Bob's encryption key), plus the corresponding bob_keys/ pair for Bob.
.PP
.B 2. Add Contacts to Keychain
.br
Each party adds their contact with their respective keys to their keychain using --add-contact.
.PP
.B 3. Encrypt Messages
.br
When Alice encrypts a message using -c alice --encrypt, the program:
.RS
\- Reads the plaintext from stdin
.br
\- XORs it with Alice's encryption key (starting from the beginning of the remaining key)
.br
\- Writes the cipher text to stdout
.br
\- Increments the EncryptedSequence number
.br
\- Updates EncryptionKeyOffset by the message length
.br
\- Sets LastMessageSentAt to the current timestamp
.br
\- Removes the used key bytes and stores only the remaining unused portion
.br
\- Saves the updated keychain to disk
.RE
.PP
.B 4. Decrypt Messages
.br
When Bob decrypts the message using -c bob --decrypt, the program:
.RS
\- Reads the cipher text from stdin
.br
\- XORs it with Bob's decryption key (which matches Alice's encryption key)
.br
\- Writes the plaintext to stdout
.br
\- Increments the DecryptedSequence number
.br
\- Updates DecryptionKeyOffset by the message length
.br
\- Sets LastMessageReceivedAt to the current timestamp
.br
\- Removes the used key bytes and stores only the remaining unused portion
.br
\- Saves the updated keychain to disk
.RE
.PP
.B 5. Perfect Forward Secrecy
.br
Because keys are consumed and removed after each use, even if the keychain is compromised, previously encrypted messages cannot be decrypted. Each message uses a unique, never-reused portion of the one-time pad.
.PP

.SS Key Consumption Example
If a contact has a 1MB (1048576 bytes) encryption key and you encrypt a 100-byte message:
.RS
\- 100 bytes are used for XOR encryption
.br
\- The remaining 1048476 bytes are stored as the new encryption key
.br
\- EncryptionKeyOffset is updated from 0 to 100
.br
\- The next encryption will use bytes 100-199 of the original key
.RE
.PP

.SH KEYCHAIN EXAMPLES

.SS Basic Keychain Operations
To add a contact without keys:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact alice
.fi
.PP
To list all contacts:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --list-contacts
.fi
.PP
To show contact details:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --show-contact alice
.fi
.PP
To check if a contact exists:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --has-contact alice && echo "Contact exists"
.fi
.PP
To remove a contact:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --remove-contact alice
.fi
.PP

.SS Complete Workflow: Secure Communication Between Alice and Bob
This example demonstrates the complete workflow for setting up and using the keychain for secure communication between two parties.

.B Step 1: Generate Key Pairs (Run on a secure machine)
.PP
Generate a 1MB key pair for Alice and Bob. This creates two directories, one per party, each holding that party's matching pair of keys:
.PP
.nf
.B cat /dev/urandom | otp --new-key-pair 1 alice bob
.fi
.PP
This creates:
.RS
\- alice_keys/encryption_for_bob.key (Alice uses to encrypt, Bob uses to decrypt)
.br
\- alice_keys/decryption_from_bob.key (Alice uses to decrypt, Bob uses to encrypt)
.br
\- bob_keys/encryption_for_alice.key (Bob uses to encrypt, Alice uses to decrypt)
.br
\- bob_keys/decryption_from_alice.key (Bob uses to decrypt, Alice uses to encrypt)
.RE
.PP
Note: alice_keys/encryption_for_bob.key and bob_keys/decryption_from_alice.key contain identical keys, as do bob_keys/encryption_for_alice.key and alice_keys/decryption_from_bob.key.

.B Step 2: Distribute Keys Securely
.PP
Transfer the keys to Alice and Bob via a secure, out-of-band method (e.g., encrypted USB drive, in-person exchange):
.RS
\- Alice receives the alice_keys/ directory (encryption_for_bob.key and decryption_from_bob.key)
.br
\- Bob receives the bob_keys/ directory (encryption_for_alice.key and decryption_from_alice.key)
.RE
.PP

.B Step 3: Alice Adds Bob to Her Keychain
.PP
On Alice's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact bob alice_keys/encryption_for_bob.key alice_keys/decryption_from_bob.key
.fi
.PP
Output:
.RS
Contact 'bob' added with keys:
.br
  Encryption key: 1048576 bytes from alice_keys/encryption_for_bob.key
.br
  Decryption key: 1048576 bytes from alice_keys/decryption_from_bob.key
.RE
.PP

.B Step 4: Bob Adds Alice to His Keychain
.PP
On Bob's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --add-contact alice bob_keys/encryption_for_alice.key bob_keys/decryption_from_alice.key
.fi
.PP
Output:
.RS
Contact 'alice' added with keys:
.br
  Encryption key: 1048576 bytes from bob_keys/encryption_for_alice.key
.br
  Decryption key: 1048576 bytes from bob_keys/decryption_from_alice.key
.RE
.PP

.B Step 5: Alice Sends an Encrypted Message to Bob
.PP
On Alice's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B echo "Hello Bob! This is a secret message." | otp -c bob --encrypt > message1.bin
.fi
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 38 bytes from encryption key for contact 'bob'
.br
Remaining encryption key: 1048538 bytes
.RE
.PP
The file message1.bin contains the cipher text. Alice sends this file to Bob through any channel (email, file transfer, etc.) since it is encrypted.

.B Step 6: Bob Decrypts Alice's Message
.PP
Bob receives message1.bin and decrypts it on his machine:
.PP
.nf
.B cat message1.bin | otp -c alice --decrypt
.fi
.PP
Output to stdout:
.RS
Hello Bob! This is a secret message.
.RE
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 38 bytes from decryption key for contact 'alice'
.br
Remaining decryption key: 1048538 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 7: Bob Sends an Encrypted Reply to Alice
.PP
On Bob's machine:
.PP
.nf
.B echo "Hi Alice! Got your message." | otp -c alice --encrypt > reply1.bin
.fi
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 27 bytes from encryption key for contact 'alice'
.br
Remaining encryption key: 1048549 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 8: Alice Decrypts Bob's Reply
.PP
Alice receives reply1.bin and decrypts it:
.PP
.nf
.B cat reply1.bin | otp -c bob --decrypt
.fi
.PP
Output to stdout:
.RS
Hi Alice! Got your message.
.RE
.PP
Stderr output:
.RS
Used 27 bytes from decryption key for contact 'bob'
.br
Remaining decryption key: 1048549 bytes
.RE
.PP

.B Step 9: Verify Contact Status
.PP
Alice can check her contact status at any time:
.PP
.nf
.B otp --show-contact bob
.fi
.PP
Output shows:
.RS
Contact: bob
.br
  EncryptionKey: ******* (1048538 bytes)
.br
  EncryptionKeyOffset: 38
.br
  EncryptedSequence: 1
.br
  DecryptionKey: ******* (1048549 bytes)
.br
  DecryptionKeyOffset: 27
.br
  DecryptedSequence: 1
.br
  RetryCount: 0
.br
  LastMessageSentAt: [timestamp]
.br
  LastMessageReceivedAt: [timestamp]
.RE
.PP
The two sequence counters are tracked separately, one per direction: Alice has sent 1 message and received 1.

.SS Important Security Notes
.RS
\- Never reuse keys. Once keys are exhausted, generate new key pairs.
.br
\- The .keychain/ directory and everything in it should be kept secure with appropriate file permissions (700 for the .keychain/ directory, 600 for the files inside it).
.br
\- Use a true hardware random number generator (TRNG) for key generation in production.
.br
\- Exchange key files through secure, out-of-band channels.
.br
\- Monitor key consumption using --show-contact to know when keys are running low.
.br
\- Be extremely careful with backups of .keychain/: restoring one rolls spent key material back to unspent and silently breaks the pad.  See SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS before backing up or restoring.
.br
\- The streaming architecture allows keys up to 1 TB in size (on 64-bit builds; 32-bit builds are limited to key files under 4 GiB \- see KEYCHAIN MANAGEMENT), enabling extremely long-term secure communication without memory constraints.
.RE
.PP

.SH ORIGIN AND ORDER VERIFICATION
Before a single key byte is spent, every decrypt verifies that the incoming message really was produced with the mirror of this contact's key \- at exactly the expected position \- and that it is exactly the next message expected in this direction.  A replayed, reordered, foreign, truncated or corrupted message is rejected with the keys untouched and a distinct exit status naming what failed.
.PP
This is achieved through a per-message metadata layer: a small binary metadata block, itself encrypted, prepended to the plaintext before the XOR pass.  Tags, length fields and values are raw binary; the overhead is typically 23 bytes of ciphertext plus a 16-byte source_id key chunk:
.PP
.RS
.nf
0x01 <source_id: 16 bytes>             a chunk of the key itself
0x02 <len: varint> <seq: len bytes>    sequence number, big-endian
0x03 <len: varint> <off: len bytes>    absolute key offset, big-endian
<plaintext...>
.fi
.RE
.PP
The length field is a ULEB128 varint (7 value bits per byte, high bit set on every byte except the last), so seq and off may be of any byte-length; values are written with the minimal number of bytes for their value, and the parser streams arbitrarily wide values in constant memory.  A well-formed value too wide to ever equal this build's expected seq/offset fails that field's validation with its own exit status; it is not treated as a malformed message.  The key range one message consumes is one contiguous run starting at the sender's current offset: first the 16-byte source_id chunk (embedded, encrypted, in the metadata \- never used as pad), then the XOR pad for the metadata+plaintext bytes.  Both sides therefore consume exactly N+16 key bytes per message, where N is the ciphertext length.
.PP
On decrypt the metadata is validated before any key material is spent: the source_id must equal the decryption key's own next 16 bytes (proving the message was produced with the mirror of this exact key at this exact position), and the sequence and offset must be exactly the ones this contact expects next.  A replayed, reordered, foreign, truncated or corrupted message is rejected \- failure reasons on stderr, nothing on stdout, no key consumed \- with the exit status naming the failed combination (see EXIT STATUS).  On success the metadata is stripped: the delivered plaintext is byte-for-byte the original message.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The crash-safety machinery guarantees that no crash, kill, power loss, disk error or concurrent invocation can make the same key range cover two messages \fBwithin one keychain\fP.  That guarantee rests on one precondition the program cannot enforce: the \fB.keychain/\fP directory must never be rolled back or duplicated.  The authority for what is still unspent is the key file's own length; nothing outside the directory records that a byte was spent, so anything that turns the clock back is invisible.
.TP
.B Restoring .keychain/ from a backup is total, silent key reuse.
Snapshot the directory, send a message, restore the snapshot, send another: both messages are encrypted with the same key range, and XORing the two ciphertexts recovers the XOR of the two plaintexts.  Exit status 0, no warning - the key files and metadata were rolled back together, so they agree with each other and the rollback checks cannot fire.  Do not snapshot, sync, or restore \fB.keychain/\fP while it is in use.  If you must back it up, treat any restore as re-keying: give every contact in the restored copy fresh key material before sending anything.
.TP
.B The same key files on two machines is the same failure by another route.
Both copies consume from offset 0 with no way to learn about each other.  Each party must hold exactly one live copy of its keys, inside its one keychain.
.TP
.B The pad is not authenticated.
One-time pad encryption provides secrecy, not integrity: flipping a ciphertext bit flips the same plaintext bit, undetectably.  If tampering matters for your use, authenticate the ciphertext by separate means.
.TP
.B Deleted working files are unlinked, not shredded.
Pending artifacts, staging files and consumed key prefixes are removed with ordinary file deletion.  On journaling or copy-on-write filesystems, and on flash media, their contents may remain recoverable from the underlying storage.  Use full-disk encryption or dedicated secure-deletion measures if this is in your threat model.
.TP
.B Redelivery restarts from the beginning.
If a crash interrupts the final delivery step partway through writing to stdout, the next run redelivers the whole message (exit status 8).  Key state stays exactly correct, but a consumer that already received part of the stream will see those bytes twice.
.PP
An attacker with write access to \fB.keychain/\fP can cause any of the above deliberately.  Key distribution, and the true randomness of the key material, remain your responsibility.

.SH EXIT STATUS
For keychain encrypt/decrypt operations
.RB ( \-c ):
.TP
.B 0
Success \- the input was encrypted or decrypted and delivered.
.TP
.B 8
A message from an interrupted earlier run was recovered and redelivered, and
.B this invocation's input was not processed.
The output is valid, but it belongs to the earlier message. Run the command again to process the input that was skipped.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; nothing was delivered and no key material was consumed.
.PP
Additionally, for
.B \-\-decrypt
the per-message metadata validation (see ORIGIN AND ORDER VERIFICATION) can reject the message before any key material is consumed; nothing is written to stdout, the failure reasons are printed to stderr, and the exit status names the exact combination that failed:
.TP
.B 1
Invalid source_id \- the message's source_id does not match the decryption key's chunk at the expected offset (shared with the generic error code).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid seq \- the message's sequence number is not the one expected next.
.TP
.B 3
Invalid offset \- the message's declared key offset is not this contact's current offset.
.TP
.B 4
Invalid source_id and seq.
.TP
.B 7
Invalid source_id and offset.
.TP
.B 6
Invalid seq and offset.
.TP
.B 5
Invalid source_id, seq and offset \- also the code for a message whose metadata cannot be parsed at all, since nothing in it can be verified: garbage, a truncated fragment, ciphertext from an incompatible or pre-metadata sender, an empty message, and input that fails with a read error before the metadata is complete all land here.
.PP
For
.B \-\-status:
.TP
.B 0
Clean and ready in both directions.
.TP
.B 4
Redelivery pending: an interrupted run left a committed message; the next operation in that direction will re-emit it (exit 8) and will not process its own input.
.TP
.B 5
Delivery confirmation outstanding: the last sent/received message has not yet been confirmed delivered; pass \fB-y\fP on the next operation once confirmed out of band.
.TP
.B 6
Key material rolled back: the key file is larger than the metadata records, so its leading bytes were already spent once.  Re-key the contact before sending or receiving anything.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (unknown contact, unreadable key file).
.PP
For
.B \-\-recover-last:
0 the copy was streamed, 2 no copy exists (nothing awaits delivery confirmation in that direction), 1 an error occurred.
.PP
For
.B \-\-xor:
0 the whole input was XORed and written, 1 an error occurred (no <bytes> argument given, <bytes> could not be opened, <bytes> was shorter than the input, or stdin/stdout failed).
.PP
All other keychain commands return 0 on success and 1 on failure, except
.B \-\-has-contact,
which returns 0 if the contact exists and 1 if it does not.

.SH EXTERNAL INTEGRATION
A program driving otp needs no library: encrypt/decrypt are stdin-to-stdout filters with distinct exit codes, and the two read-only query commands answer everything a client must know before its next operation.  Per contact, the loop is:
.RS
.br
1. \fBotp --status <contact> --porcelain\fP.  Exit 4: run the operation with empty input to redeliver the recovered message (exit 8), transmit or deliver it, ack it.  Exit 5: wait for the peer's acknowledgement; if the transport lost the message, re-transmit the same bytes from \fBotp --recover-last <contact> --sent\fP (never re-encrypt), or on the receive side re-deliver the plaintext from \fB--recover-last <contact> --received\fP.
.br
2. Run the operation with \fB-y\fP - the client's assertion, licensed by the peer's acknowledgement of the previous message, that delivery was confirmed.  Exit 0: transmit/deliver, keep a copy until acked.  Exit 8: the output is a recovered earlier message and the input was not processed - handle it, then re-run.  Exit 2-7: the incoming message failed metadata validation (wrong source, order or offset) and was rejected with no key consumed - do not ack it; investigate the channel.  Exit 1: nothing was spent; safe to retry.
.br
3. On the receive side, send the acknowledgement only after the decrypted plaintext reached the application intact - that acknowledgement is what licenses the sender's next \fB-y\fP.
.RE
.PP
Re-transmission always uses the stored ciphertext bytes, never a fresh encryption, so no key range is ever spent twice for one message and the receiver's key position stays aligned.  Both query commands are strictly read-only, so every crash-safety and key-reuse protection described above applies unchanged.  The full flow, with the recovery ladder, is in the "External Integrations" section of README.md.

